JIT: an erased block's entry points exit to the dispatcher instead of running the old translation

JavaScriptCore's JIT (Bun 1.4.4 for Windows x64; the Coherent GT gauges of Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 run the
same engine) rewrites code it already ran: its compiler threads write new code on a page while the main thread
repatches jumps there from C++ called by that code (FTL lazy slow paths, OSR exits, tier-up). Under FEX about one run
in twenty asserted in JSC (LazySlowPath::generate's RELEASE_ASSERT(!m_stub), OSRExitStream::at,
operationTriggerTierUpNow) and ended with 0xC0000409: the main thread was running a translation made before the
rewrite. Highball's FEX without squark's patches does it too; the Windows ARM64 build of the same Bun under the same
Wine does not, and with JSC's concurrent JIT off it stops.

A write fault on a code page erases the page's blocks from the map and makes the page writable, so later writes there
do not fault. Host code can still lead into an erased block: a call-return stack prediction, an L1 slot another
thread had already read, a link being severed while it runs. When the guest has rewritten the block's bytes by then,
that path runs the old code.

Each entry point now starts with a nop, and the unit gets one exit stub per entry point after its code: store the
entry's RIP, clear the thread's L1 slot if it still holds that RIP, jump to the dispatcher loop. GuestToHostMap::Erase
turns the nop into "b <exit>"; AddBlockMapping turns it back when Highball's smc-retire reinstates the block (ARM
allows a B/NOP swap while other cores execute it). SQUARK_FEX_STALE_EXIT=0 turns this off, 2 limits it to units
compiled from writable memory.

The dispatcher's compile call (CompileBlockEnter) enters a unit it has just compiled or reinstated past the nop. That
unit was checked against the guest's bytes under CodeInvalidationMutex; a write that invalidates it right after waits
for that lock and lands after the invalidation, so running it this once is the same as having fetched it before the
write, as FEX always did. Through the nop, a thread calling a function while another thread kept storing data beside
it on the page lost that race nearly every time and translated the function again about a hundred times per call
(Highball's smcretire, its data-writing round: 1.0 s -> 89 s).

squark tools/smc-subpage S9 (a function calls C, which rewrites the instruction the call returns to, while another
thread stores data on the page), 10,000 calls: 3,064-3,813 return the old value with Highball's FEX, 0038, 0039 or
SQUARK_FEX_STALE_EXIT=0, none with this (1.5 s -> 1.8-1.9 s). JSC stress (Bun 1.4.4 x64, FBW A380X ND gauge, low
tier-up thresholds, same DLL): 8/40 runs crash with it off, 0/40 on. smcretire's data-writing round 0.96 s, as 0039.
Alternated against 0039: TFDi MD-11 WebAssembly replay +3.4% instructions, cycles per frame 9.38/9.64/9.55M against
9.39/9.66/9.64M, state hash unchanged; FBW A380X 2.61M -> 2.64M instructions, 480k/479k against 472k/480k cycles per
tick; the A380X's 24 HTML gauges in Bun with JIT on, text hashes unchanged, frame total 4.30-4.61 ms against
4.25-4.56 ms.
